Comments explain reasons and constraints
// begins a comment through the end of the line. /* and */ delimit a block comment; block comments do not nest. Inside a string literal, "//" is ordinary text. A comment is not executed, but removing it carelessly can join separate tokens. Keep the intended token boundary.
“Multiply by 60” repeats a visible operation. “The schedule records whole minutes; the report requires seconds” explains a unit contract. A comment about equipment explains why two seats are reserved. When policy changes, review both the comment and value. An obsolete explanation can make a wrong formula seem justified.

Reading your first C++ program
#include <iostream> makes stream declarations available. int main() is the entry point; braces enclose its body. int price = 120 creates an integer variable with an initial value. The = operator assigns a value; it does not test equality. A simple statement ends with a semicolon.
std::cin >> price >> count reads two whitespace-separated integers. std::cout << total prints the result; \n ends the line. If the stream cannot read a number, !(std::cin >> ...) is true and the program prints invalid. The || operator means logical OR: one failed boundary condition is enough to reject the input.
